[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Rubinstein System, Rubinstein Variation, Main Line"]

{Nimzo-Indian Defense: Rubinstein System, Rubinstein Variation, Main Line. A slight inaccuracy in move order — the bishop is better placed before this pawn commits.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 c5 5. Ne2 cxd4 {Book so far. The plan is the usual one: finish development, then decide which side to open.} 6. exd4 O-O 7. a3 Be7 8. d5 ! exd5 {The critical moment. The pieces are as good as they are going to get; something has to happen now.} (8... e5 Rb1 e4 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. cxd5 Re8 10. d6 Bf8 11. g3 Qb6 ?! 12. Bg2 Bxd6 13. Be3 Qa6 14. O-O Be5 15. Re1 d5 16. Bg5 Be6 17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Nf4 Bxc3 19. bxc3 Nc6 20. Nxe6 fxe6 21. Qg4 Na5 22. Re3 Nc4 23. Rxe6 Qxe6 24. Qxe6+ Rxe6 25. Bxd5 Kf7 26. Re1 Re8 27. Bxc4 b5 28. Bxe6+ Rxe6 29. Rb1 a6 30. Kf1 Rc6 31. Rc1 h5 32. Ke2 g5 33. f3 h4 34. Rc2 Rc4 35. Kd3 Ra4 36. Ra2 Ke7 37. Re2+ Kf6 38. Ra2 Ke7 39. Re2+ Kf6 40. Ra2 {The rest is technique, which is another way of saying it still has to be played accurately.} Ke7 1/2-1/2

[Event "Ruy Lopez - the Berlin: Chapter 2"]
[ChapterName "The critical sideline"]
