{King's Indian Defense: Fianchetto Variation, Double Fianchetto Attack. Book so far. The plan is the usual one: finish development, then decide which side to open.}
1. g3 g6 2. Bg2 Bg7 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. d4 O-O 5. O-O d6 {A slight inaccuracy in move order — the bishop is better placed before this pawn commits.} 6. c4 Nbd7 7. b3 e5 8. Nc3 ! Re8 {Prophylaxis. The move looks slow, and it takes away every active idea the opponent had.} (8... exd4 Rb1 dxc3 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. e3 c6 10. Bb2 e4 11. Ng5 d5 ?! 12. cxd5 cxd5 13. a4 Nf8 14. Qd2 h6 15. Nh3 g5 16. Ba3 Ng6 17. a5 Bg4 18. Rfb1 Qd7 19. Qe1 Bxh3 20. Bxh3 Qxh3 21. Qf1 Qe6 22. Nb5 Qd7 23. Rc1 Rec8 24. Rxc8+ Rxc8 25. Nxa7 Ra8 26. Nb5 Rxa5 27. Nc3 h5 28. b4 Ra8 29. b5 h4 30. Bc5 Rxa1 31. Qxa1 h3 32. Na4 Qxb5 33. Bb6 Bf8 34. Ba7 Be7 35. Qc1 {Precise to the end. A pawn move here instead would have let the defence build a fortress.} Qxa4 0-1
